    <h1>Christian Howard</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="prose" aria-label="Biography">
    <p class="lede">Dr. Christian Howard is a trombonist and educator based in the Twin Cities, active across orchestral, chamber, and solo settings on alto, tenor, and bass trombone and euphonium.</p>

    <h2>Performance</h2>

    <p>Christian has held the second trombone chair with the Duluth Superior Symphony Orchestra since October 2023, and has performed as a substitute with the Minnesota Orchestra since February 2023.</p>

    <p>He has been a finalist or semifinalist in five national auditions over the past seven years, most recently as a tenor trombone finalist for the United States Army Band &ldquo;Pershing&rsquo;s Own&rdquo; Ceremonial Band in March 2026. He was a bass trombone semifinalist for the same ensemble in March 2025 and a bass trombone finalist in May 2024, a semifinalist for the United States Navy Concert Band in 2020, and a finalist for The Orchestra Now in 2019.</p>

    <p>His broader performing record includes Symphony in C, LoftOpera, the Chautauqua Symphony Orchestra, the Chautauqua Music School Festival Orchestra, and the Compass Rose Brass Ensemble. He has appeared as a soloist with the University of Northwestern Symphonic Band and as a guest artist at the Texas State Trombone Symposium, and has participated in the Third Coast Trombone Retreat, the Cleveland Trombone Seminar, and the Atlantic Brass Quintet Seminar.</p>

    <h2>Teaching</h2>

    <p>Christian is Visiting Lecturer of Trombone and Euphonium at the University of Minnesota, where he leads the trombone and euphonium studio as primary teacher of record for undergraduate and graduate applied students. He directs and coaches the trombone choir, runs studio class, serves on juries and recital committees, and leads outreach performances. For three years he was the sole instructor of record for Brass Methods at the University of Minnesota, teaching trumpet, horn, trombone, euphonium, and tuba.</p>

    <p>Students he has taught, coached, or mentored have gone on to a position in a United States Navy fleet band and to study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice University, Carnegie Mellon University, and DePaul University. <a href="/teaching/">More on teaching and student outcomes</a>.</p>

    <h2>Research</h2>

    <p>Christian&rsquo;s doctoral work is a set of trombone transcriptions of three songs from Ralph Vaughan Williams&rsquo;s <i>Songs of Travel</i> &mdash; &ldquo;The Vagabond,&rdquo; &ldquo;Whither Must I Wander,&rdquo; and &ldquo;The Roadside Fire&rdquo; &mdash; together with a study of how vocal idiom translates to the instrument. The project treats diction and articulation, text painting, rubato, and phrasing as interpretive problems rather than technical ones. The transcriptions and the accompanying paper are complete.</p>

